'''''El País''''' ({{lang-bl|The Country}}) is a multi-language {{W|Collaborative journalism|collaborative}} [[w:Newspaper|daily newspaper]] in [[Balisca]]. ''El País'' is based in the city of [[Yerba Buena]]. Its headquarters and central editorial staff are all located there, although there are regional offices in several other Baliscan cities ([[Alessandria]], [[Navassa]], [[Macaio]], [[Axarquía]], and [[Castello]]) where regional editions have been produced since 2015. It operates largely on the principles of {{W|Open-source journalism|open source and wiki journalism}}.

The editor-in-chief Erik Bruckner succeeded Otávia Moreira in 2015. Since 2018, the paper's main newsprint sections have been published in tabloid format. As of July 2021, its print edition had a daily circulation of 831,000. The newspaper has an online edition, elpais.coop.bl, as well as two international websites, [[El País del Sur]] (founded in 2013) and [[El País del Nord]] (founded in 2011). The paper's readership is generally reflective of Baliscan society, and the term "pais reader" or "''paisans''" is used to imply a stereotype of radical, left-wing militant views.

Despite its independence, it has been criticized internationally as "sensationalist", especially in relation to the [[Baliscan duo|Balisca–West Pacific crisis]] in which it was denounced as a "[[Zavier Bilal|pro-Bilal]] mouthpiece" by prominent Pasifikan officials.

In a [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) research poll in September 2018 designed to interrogate the public's trust of specific titles online, ''El País'' scored highest for digital-content news, with 85% of readers agreeing that they "trust what [they] see in it". A December 2018 report of a poll by the Publishers Audience Measurement Committee (PAMC) stated that the paper's print edition was found to be the most trusted in Balisca in the period from October 2016 to September 2018. It was also reported to be the most-read of Balisca's "quality newsbrands", including digital editions; other "quality" brands include [[El Spíritu del Sur|The Spirit of the South]], [[South Atlantic Times|The South Atlantic Times]], and [[Die Baliskäner|''Die Baliskäner'']] ("The Baliscan"). While El País' print circulation is in decline, the report indicated that news from the paper, including that reported online, reaches more than 60 million Baliscan adults each month.

